213
00:08:30,705 --> 00:08:32,875
On the map, it becomes clear why

214
00:08:32,895 --> 00:08:35,129
this lake was such a deadly trap.

215
00:08:35,149 --> 00:08:37,269
The dammed waters of the upper Pukhan

216
00:08:37,289 --> 00:08:39,944
River lay stretched across the land,

217
00:08:39,964 --> 00:08:42,507
reaching east toward Yanggu.

218
00:08:42,692 --> 00:08:43,865
The Chinese were mostly

219
00:08:43,885 --> 00:08:46,062
infantry moving on foot.

220
00:08:46,122 --> 00:08:48,288
They had almost no boats to cross water,

221
00:08:48,308 --> 00:08:50,984
and almost no equipment to build bridges.

222
00:08:51,080 --> 00:08:52,734
Crossing a deep lake with nothing but

223
00:08:52,754 --> 00:08:55,469
their bodies was practically impossible.

224
00:08:55,664 --> 00:08:58,023
In the end, the way north funneled

225
00:08:58,043 --> 00:09:00,357
into a single road passing the Hwachon

226
00:09:00,377 --> 00:09:03,201
Dam at the reservoir's western tip.

227
00:09:03,318 --> 00:09:06,041
Past the dam, through Hwachon,

228
00:09:06,061 --> 00:09:08,742
and on north toward Chorwon.

229
00:09:08,924 --> 00:09:11,454
South of the dam rose high mountains such

230
00:09:11,474 --> 00:09:14,435
as Mount Yonghwa and Mount Pyongpung.

231
00:09:14,525 --> 00:09:16,884
The valleys and roads between them narrowed

232
00:09:16,904 --> 00:09:19,080
the closer they came to the lakeshore.

233
00:09:19,161 --> 00:09:20,962
If tens of thousands crowded in

234
00:09:20,982 --> 00:09:23,204
at once, the terrain could only

235
00:09:23,224 --> 00:09:25,430
jam like the neck of a bottle.

236
00:09:25,621 --> 00:09:27,571
Groups pouring down several valleys

237
00:09:27,591 --> 00:09:30,109
from the south all converged at last

238
00:09:30,129 --> 00:09:32,692
onto the one road along the lakeshore.

239
00:09:32,807 --> 00:09:34,563
Where the water spread wide,

240
00:09:34,583 --> 00:09:37,577
the river of men was narrowing instead.

241
00:09:37,770 --> 00:09:40,010
Cut the Hwachon terrain into a height

242
00:09:40,030 --> 00:09:42,548
profile, and this is what you see.

243
00:09:42,624 --> 00:09:45,529
High ridges to the south, narrow valleys below

244
00:09:45,549 --> 00:09:48,579
them, and deep water blocking the north.

245
00:09:48,698 --> 00:09:50,409
Ground that offered no good way

246
00:09:50,429 --> 00:09:53,104
forward, back, or to either side.

247
00:09:53,292 --> 00:09:55,134
In truth, both sides had been

248
00:09:55,154 --> 00:09:57,603
fighting over this dam for some time.

249
00:09:57,650 --> 00:09:59,748
In early April the Chinese opened its

250
00:09:59,768 --> 00:10:02,570
floodgates to send water surging downstream,

251
00:10:02,590 --> 00:10:05,883
and on May 1st U.S. Navy torpedo planes

252
00:10:05,903 --> 00:10:08,693
even launched torpedoes at its gates.

253
00:10:08,880 --> 00:10:10,761
Douglas Skyraider attack planes

254
00:10:10,781 --> 00:10:12,252
wrecked one floodgate with

255
00:10:12,272 --> 00:10:14,573
torpedoes and damaged another.

256
00:10:14,640 --> 00:10:16,384
It is counted as almost the only

257
00:10:16,404 --> 00:10:18,114
time aerial torpedoes were

258
00:10:18,134 --> 00:10:20,708
actually used in the Korean War.

259
00:10:20,896 --> 00:10:22,791
The corps decided quickly.

260
00:10:22,921 --> 00:10:24,824
Leave only the 19th Regiment and the

261
00:10:24,844 --> 00:10:26,884
2nd Battalion of the 2nd Regiment

262
00:10:26,904 --> 00:10:29,058
at the Chiam-ni ring, and rush the

263
00:10:29,078 --> 00:10:31,505
rest of the 6th Division to Chunchon.

264
00:10:31,611 --> 00:10:34,892
Then, side by side with the U.S. 7th Division,

265
00:10:34,912 --> 00:10:37,596
drive north toward the Hwachon Dam.

266
00:10:37,789 --> 00:10:39,984
That night, the 3rd Battalion of

267
00:10:40,004 --> 00:10:41,839
the 2nd Regiment set out on a

268
00:10:41,859 --> 00:10:44,183
night march over rugged mountains.

269
00:10:44,269 --> 00:10:46,560
Following ridgelines without trails,

270
00:10:46,580 --> 00:10:47,573
they had to reach the

271
00:10:47,593 --> 00:10:49,710
lakeshore before the enemy did.

272
00:10:49,900 --> 00:10:51,764
Outrun the enemy you are chasing,

273
00:10:51,784 --> 00:10:54,323
and block his path before he gets there.

274
00:10:54,343 --> 00:10:56,510
This was the moment the pursuit

275
00:10:56,530 --> 00:10:59,602
turned into a vast encirclement.

276
00:10:59,924 --> 00:11:01,629
On the morning of May 27th,

277
00:11:01,649 --> 00:11:04,033
the order to attack came down.

278
00:11:04,230 --> 00:11:05,945
The 2nd Regiment's objective was

279
00:11:05,965 --> 00:11:07,640
the Hwachon power plant and

280
00:11:07,660 --> 00:11:10,099
Maebong peak just south of it.

281
00:11:10,189 --> 00:11:12,230
The 7th Regiment's objective was

282
00:11:12,250 --> 00:11:13,630
the ridgeline running from

283
00:11:13,650 --> 00:11:16,942
Mount Puyong to Mount Pyongpung.

284
00:11:17,124 --> 00:11:18,781
Maebong was the peak overlooking

285
00:11:18,801 --> 00:11:20,669
the dam and the power plant.

286
00:11:20,758 --> 00:11:22,981
Whoever held it could see, and shell,

287
00:11:23,001 --> 00:11:25,997
everything moving along the lakeshore road.

288
00:11:26,191 --> 00:11:28,053
The men of the 2nd Regiment stormed

289
00:11:28,073 --> 00:11:30,386
up the steep slopes toward Maebong.

290
00:11:30,446 --> 00:11:31,735
And they retook both the

291
00:11:31,755 --> 00:11:33,556
power plant and the peak.

292
00:11:33,603 --> 00:11:35,837
At the western tip of the lake, the cork

293
00:11:35,857 --> 00:11:38,101
had been driven into the escape route.

294
00:11:38,282 --> 00:11:40,455
For the Chinese, losing the power

295
00:11:40,475 --> 00:11:42,514
plant meant losing their way out.

296
00:11:42,631 --> 00:11:44,125
Now any group approaching the

297
00:11:44,145 --> 00:11:45,911
dam became a target in plain

298
00:11:45,931 --> 00:11:48,072
sight from the top of Maebong.

299
00:11:48,255 --> 00:11:50,290
At the same time, the 7th Regiment

300
00:11:50,310 --> 00:11:52,440
pushed along the ridges to the east.

301
00:11:52,462 --> 00:11:55,309
And on the main road from Chunchon to Hwachon,

302
00:11:55,329 --> 00:11:58,546
the U.S. 7th Division was coming north.

303
00:11:58,752 --> 00:12:01,108
The soldiers of the U.S. 7th Division also

304
00:12:01,128 --> 00:12:03,542
climbed these mountain roads on foot.

305
00:12:03,570 --> 00:12:06,282
Where trucks could not go, all they had were

306
00:12:06,302 --> 00:12:09,781
their legs and the packs on their backs.

307
00:12:09,962 --> 00:12:11,819
Now the picture was complete.

308
00:12:11,908 --> 00:12:14,574
The ROK 6th Division blocked the western

309
00:12:14,594 --> 00:12:16,384
and southern ridges, and the U.S.

310
00:12:16,404 --> 00:12:19,400
7th Division the main road to the south.

311
00:12:19,493 --> 00:12:21,629
And to the north lay the deep lake.

312
00:12:21,811 --> 00:12:24,393
A ring sealed by gun barrels on three

313
00:12:24,413 --> 00:12:26,793
sides and by water on the fourth.

314
00:12:26,814 --> 00:12:29,276
The lake itself had become the fourth wall.

315
00:12:29,487 --> 00:12:31,709
Then on May 28th, American

316
00:12:31,729 --> 00:12:34,019
troops took the town of Hwachon.

317
00:12:34,147 --> 00:12:36,289
South Korean troops pushed all the way

318
00:12:36,309 --> 00:12:38,702
to the southern edge of the reservoir.

319
00:12:38,729 --> 00:12:41,065
For the Chinese crowded on the lakeshore,

320
00:12:41,085 --> 00:12:43,660
even the last way out had been shut.

321
00:12:43,848 --> 00:12:45,983
The artillery took aim at every group

322
00:12:46,003 --> 00:12:48,053
moving along the lakeshore road.

323
00:12:48,179 --> 00:12:49,980
When observers on Maebong called

324
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:52,063
in coordinates, shells landed

325
00:12:52,083 --> 00:12:53,881
on that road moments later.

326
00:12:54,223 --> 00:12:56,667
And on that road, one of the most horrific

327
00:12:56,687 --> 00:12:59,714
scenes of the Korean War began to unfold.

328
00:12:59,916 --> 00:13:02,360
The three days of fighting from May 27th

329
00:13:02,380 --> 00:13:05,022
to the 29th were a horrific slaughter.

330
00:13:05,122 --> 00:13:07,196
With their chain of command collapsed,

331
00:13:07,216 --> 00:13:09,939
the Chinese surged onto the lakeshore road

332
00:13:09,959 --> 00:13:13,090
in groups of hundreds, even thousands.

333
00:13:13,273 --> 00:13:15,006
Seen through the eyes of a Chinese

334
00:13:15,026 --> 00:13:16,964
soldier standing on that road,

335
00:13:16,984 --> 00:13:19,717
this is what it must have looked like.

336
00:13:19,774 --> 00:13:22,566
Ahead, water that could not be crossed.

337
00:13:22,586 --> 00:13:25,872
Behind, shells falling without end.

338
00:13:25,982 --> 00:13:27,374
And from the heights above,

339
00:13:27,394 --> 00:13:30,468
South Korean rifles looking down.

340
00:13:30,650 --> 00:13:32,282
Columns trying to slip out toward

341
00:13:32,302 --> 00:13:33,955
Chorwon were caught in plain

342
00:13:33,975 --> 00:13:36,538
view of 6th Division observers.

343
00:13:36,693 --> 00:13:39,519
Shells, machine guns, and fighter planes

344
00:13:39,539 --> 00:13:42,133
diving out of the sky fell upon them.

345
00:13:42,324 --> 00:13:44,569
Among the men driven into this dead end,

346
00:13:44,589 --> 00:13:47,061
some threw themselves into the lake.

347
00:13:47,116 --> 00:13:49,656
They were trying to swim to the far shore.

348
00:13:49,747 --> 00:13:51,525
But unable to cross the deep,

349
00:13:51,545 --> 00:13:54,598
wide water, many drowned, and others were

350
00:13:54,618 --> 00:13:57,193
killed by American air strikes.

351
00:13:57,383 --> 00:13:59,257
There was no will left to fight.

352
00:13:59,311 --> 00:14:01,227
Not only platoons and companies,

353
00:14:01,247 --> 00:14:04,151
but whole battalions reportedly came walking

354
00:14:04,171 --> 00:14:07,111
out in lines with their hands raised.

355
00:14:07,301 --> 00:14:08,397
Many of the prisoners had

356
00:14:08,417 --> 00:14:10,444
not eaten anything for days.

357
00:14:10,509 --> 00:14:12,800
There are accounts of them wolfing down rice

358
00:14:12,820 --> 00:14:15,815
balls handed over by South Korean soldiers.

359
00:14:15,983 --> 00:14:17,330
The enemy who had been aiming a

360
00:14:17,350 --> 00:14:19,299
rifle moments before had become,

361
00:14:19,319 --> 00:14:22,650
once again, just a hungry young man.

362
00:14:22,850 --> 00:14:25,399
The abandoned equipment was enormous too.

363
00:14:25,419 --> 00:14:27,459
Dozens of truckloads of supplies and

364
00:14:27,479 --> 00:14:30,464
weapons were left lying by the roadside.

365
00:14:30,658 --> 00:14:32,584
Days later, countless bodies

366
00:14:32,604 --> 00:14:34,393
began to surface on the lake.

367
00:14:34,483 --> 00:14:36,763
Kim Dal-yuk, who passed through here as

368
00:14:36,783 --> 00:14:39,165
commander of 6th Company, 2nd Battalion,

369
00:14:39,185 --> 00:14:42,972
7th Regiment, 6th Division, recalled it this way.

370
00:14:43,156 --> 00:14:45,840
The whole reservoir area reeked so badly

371
00:14:45,860 --> 00:14:47,957
of rotting corpses that they marched

372
00:14:47,977 --> 00:14:50,370
with garlic stuffed in their noses.

373
00:14:50,538 --> 00:14:52,146
And that the blue waters of Paro

374
00:14:52,166 --> 00:14:53,534
Lake had turned red,

375
00:14:53,554 --> 00:14:55,954
with bodies drifting on the surface.

376
00:14:56,147 --> 00:14:57,177
So how many Chinese

377
00:14:57,197 --> 00:14:58,964
soldiers fell here in all?

378
00:14:59,111 --> 00:15:02,175
Drawing on U.S. IX Corps command reports,

379
00:15:02,195 --> 00:15:04,611
the ROK Army's Military History

380
00:15:04,631 --> 00:15:06,614
Institute put the figure at thirty-two

381
00:15:06,634 --> 00:15:09,619
thousand and forty-six killed or captured.

382
00:15:09,809 --> 00:15:12,352
That figure is not the 6th Division's alone,

383
00:15:12,372 --> 00:15:14,401
but the result of all of U.S.

384
00:15:14,421 --> 00:15:17,507
IX Corps around Chiam-ni and Paro Lake.

385
00:15:17,593 --> 00:15:20,168
The Encyclopedia of Korean Culture puts

386
00:15:20,188 --> 00:15:22,832
Chinese casualties at Paro Lake at

387
00:15:22,852 --> 00:15:24,975
no fewer than twenty-five thousand.

388
00:15:25,172 --> 00:15:27,340
The Chinese side, by contrast,

389
00:15:27,360 --> 00:15:29,822
is reported to have recorded only around

390
00:15:29,842 --> 00:15:32,189
three thousand dead in this battle.

391
00:15:32,265 --> 00:15:34,101
Victors' records tend to swell,

392
00:15:34,121 --> 00:15:36,929
and the losers' to shrink. The exact number

393
00:15:36,949 --> 00:15:39,737
still lies submerged beneath the lake.

394
00:15:39,916 --> 00:15:41,216
One thing is certain.

395
00:15:41,296 --> 00:15:43,029
The division that had collapsed a month

396
00:15:43,049 --> 00:15:45,317
earlier stood at the spearhead of the final

397
00:15:45,337 --> 00:15:48,276
blow that ended the enemy's May Offensive.

398
00:15:48,376 --> 00:15:50,405
The Chinese spring offensive effectively

399
00:15:50,425 --> 00:15:52,860
came to an end on these shores.

400
00:15:53,200 --> 00:15:55,687
And this lake would be given a new name.

401
00:15:55,902 --> 00:15:57,768
President Syngman Rhee gave the

402
00:15:57,788 --> 00:16:00,505
Hwachon Reservoir the name Paroho.

403
00:16:00,709 --> 00:16:03,445
Pa, to smash. Ro, a captive,

404
00:16:03,465 --> 00:16:06,437
a barbarian. Ho, a lake.

405
00:16:06,487 --> 00:16:07,606
It means the lake where the

406
00:16:07,626 --> 00:16:09,733
barbarians were smashed.

407
00:16:09,940 --> 00:16:11,925
When it was first formed, the lake had

408
00:16:11,945 --> 00:16:15,340
sometimes been called Taebung Lake, because

409
00:16:15,360 --> 00:16:18,401
its shape resembled a giant mythical bird.

410
00:16:18,548 --> 00:16:20,861
After the war, it was renamed to

411
00:16:20,881 --> 00:16:22,799
carry the memory of a victory.

412
00:16:22,981 --> 00:16:26,321
In November 1955, a monument bearing the

413
00:16:26,341 --> 00:16:30,042
name Paroho was unveiled on the lakeshore.

414
00:16:30,142 --> 00:16:32,045
Today a security exhibition hall

415
00:16:32,065 --> 00:16:34,527
and a pavilion stand on that hill,

416
00:16:34,547 --> 00:16:36,300
overlooking the whole lake.

417
00:16:36,486 --> 00:16:39,051
A few years ago, a proposal to restore the

418
00:16:39,071 --> 00:16:43,046
old name, Taebung Lake, stirred controversy.

419
00:16:43,126 --> 00:16:44,745
But amid opposition from local

420
00:16:44,765 --> 00:16:46,919
veterans and residents, the lake

421
00:16:46,939 --> 00:16:49,712
still keeps the name Paroho today.

422
00:16:49,910 --> 00:16:51,942
So how could a division that had collapsed

423
00:16:51,962 --> 00:16:54,458
only a month earlier become the driving force

424
00:16:54,478 --> 00:16:56,813
that trapped an army of thirty thousand?

425
00:16:56,961 --> 00:16:58,671
Let's look at four reasons.

426
00:16:58,872 --> 00:17:02,418
First, a division that fell and rose again.

427
00:17:02,599 --> 00:17:04,814
The humiliation of Sachang-ni left the

428
00:17:04,834 --> 00:17:07,443
men determined never to retreat again.

429
00:17:07,502 --> 00:17:10,066
That resolve was proven at Yongmunsan,

430
00:17:10,086 --> 00:17:12,558
and the confidence it gave them carried their

431
00:17:12,578 --> 00:17:15,964
exhausted legs sixty kilometers further.

432
00:17:16,153 --> 00:17:18,684
Second, a pursuit that never stopped.

433
00:17:18,871 --> 00:17:20,495
They gave a crumbling enemy no

434
00:17:20,515 --> 00:17:22,349
chance to catch its breath.

435
00:17:22,518 --> 00:17:24,377
Denying the enemy the time to dig

436
00:17:24,397 --> 00:17:27,313
in and make a stand again: that was

437
00:17:27,333 --> 00:17:29,093
the true meaning of the pursuit.

438
00:17:29,281 --> 00:17:32,082
Third, the lake as the fourth wall.

439
00:17:32,294 --> 00:17:33,770
Commanders did not miss the fact

440
00:17:33,790 --> 00:17:35,238
that the lake funneled the enemy's

441
00:17:35,258 --> 00:17:37,503
retreat into a single road.

442
00:17:37,586 --> 00:17:39,729
And they seized that one chokepoint,

443
00:17:39,749 --> 00:17:42,028
the Hwachon Dam and Maebong,

444
00:17:42,048 --> 00:17:44,717
first. The side that read the terrain

445
00:17:44,737 --> 00:17:47,054
decided the shape of the battle.

446
00:17:47,240 --> 00:17:50,212
Fourth, one week of supplies.

447
00:17:50,421 --> 00:17:51,908
A starving army could neither

448
00:17:51,928 --> 00:17:54,400
march fast nor hold out long.

449
00:17:54,520 --> 00:17:56,367
The Chinese offensive broke the moment

450
00:17:56,387 --> 00:17:58,564
its supplies ran dry, and that

451
00:17:58,584 --> 00:18:00,787
was exactly when the pursuit began.

452
00:18:00,984 --> 00:18:01,997
To sum it up.

453
00:18:02,039 --> 00:18:03,739
The will of soldiers who had risen

454
00:18:03,759 --> 00:18:06,162
again, legs that never rested,

455
00:18:06,182 --> 00:18:08,734
and eyes that read the terrain.

456
00:18:08,773 --> 00:18:11,550
And a starving enemy. All four came

457
00:18:11,570 --> 00:18:13,745
together at once on a single lakeshore.

458
00:18:13,936 --> 00:18:16,704
Of course, this victory has its shadows too.

459
00:18:16,809 --> 00:18:18,850
As it later emerged, much of the

460
00:18:18,870 --> 00:18:21,115
withdrawing enemy force the Americans had

461
00:18:21,135 --> 00:18:23,448
targeted had reportedly already slipped

462
00:18:23,468 --> 00:18:26,033
through the dam passage by the 27th,

463
00:18:26,053 --> 00:18:28,773
when the 6th Division's attack began.

464
00:18:28,953 --> 00:18:31,583
The figures also vary widely depending

465
00:18:31,603 --> 00:18:34,644
on who counted, when, and how far.

466
00:18:34,681 --> 00:18:36,847
That is why some researchers treat this battle

467
00:18:36,867 --> 00:18:39,374
separately from the Chiam-ni encirclement

468
00:18:39,394 --> 00:18:42,023
and the fight for the Hwachon power plant.

469
00:18:42,203 --> 00:18:43,920
The war ended, and the

470
00:18:43,940 --> 00:18:45,831
lake grew quiet again.

471
00:18:45,940 --> 00:18:48,219
Today, mist rises over Paro Lake

472
00:18:48,239 --> 00:18:50,086
every early morning, and fishing

473
00:18:50,106 --> 00:18:52,395
boats drift lazily on the water.

474
00:18:52,581 --> 00:18:54,129
But beneath those waters lie

475
00:18:54,149 --> 00:18:56,904
countless men who never made it home.

476
00:18:56,991 --> 00:18:58,417
Whatever their nationality,

477
00:18:58,437 --> 00:19:00,750
most of them were young soldiers who

478
00:19:00,770 --> 00:19:03,065
did not even leave a name behind.

479
00:19:03,253 --> 00:19:05,316
Those who fought to hold this lake,

480
00:19:05,336 --> 00:19:07,819
and those who tried to cross it.

481
00:19:07,898 --> 00:19:10,063
More than seventy years later, the water

482
00:19:10,083 --> 00:19:13,434
flows on in silence, holding all of them.

483
00:19:13,767 --> 00:19:16,567
A defeated division rose again, and a victorious

484
00:19:16,587 --> 00:19:18,825
army collapsed at the edge of a lake.

485
00:19:18,845 --> 00:19:21,750
The Lake of the Defeated Barbarians.

486
00:19:21,770 --> 00:19:23,577
Behind that name lies the

487
00:19:23,597 --> 00:19:25,465
water that swallowed it all.

488
00:19:25,638 --> 00:19:27,398
If this story meant something to you,

489
00:19:27,418 --> 00:19:29,970
please join us with a subscribe and a like.

490
00:19:30,060 --> 00:19:32,107
We will continue the fading records

491
00:19:32,127 --> 00:19:34,109
of this war in the next video.
